                      <p class=3D"corner-intro">A collection of web design =
and development news and thoughts from CodePen's own Chris Coyier.</p>=0D=
=0A                          </mj-raw>=0D=0A                          <h3>S=
hare What You Do</h3>=0A<p>I=E2=80=99ve always loved a good blog post of so=
meone just saying what they use and do. Keerthana Krishnan has a good one h=
ere with <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/EWwHPhIYI4Vg166wi58I2=
Q~~/AABEfhA~/NkgYFsTxUWPdJxellSOVNVWrdv_T7wAnHkIA_4UrVLVpMk8bs8HETvFdCn2z4N=
9kZ66vaSpYNTIMPiDHCmlLCmA-fU5gAyVH0xGahbeIySZaTESIS4BDb71FQ85QbkonpAdFyxbE5=
r8ufVuayfauTg60N2ytSFzOkdGXBY9y5Fi2n7m_TBc9nTWPOReDnVMh">Chrome DevTools Fe=
atures I Use All the Time (and Why You Should Too)</a>. Cheeky parenthetica=
ls, Keerthana. Like, sure, I=E2=80=99ll read a blog post about new Chrome D=
evTools features, but I=E2=80=99m more interested in a real person who uses=
 the tech regularly and what they actually do to be productive. Keerthana f=
ocuses on a bunch of performance features, which makes sense since it was a=
 post on a performance blog. I like the user flows thing (which I=E2=80=99v=
e never tried!). It=E2=80=99s like a Lighthouse test except for more than a=
 static page load:</p>=0A<blockquote><p>But users don=E2=80=99t experience =
websites as static pages. They click, type, navigate, and wait.</p></blockq=
uote>=0A<p>People=E2=80=99s lived experiences. Gold. Here=E2=80=99s Emil Ko=
walski=E2=80=99s postmortem on building what turned out to be an ultra-popu=
lar component (<a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/_Zm8LK7Qth6TMie=
vLCfwuQ~~/AABEfhA~/D2m8u_wcTp45eh_DYP6ILNBgFEjDCD7OwNhqEO3CcNLJYYxpNihSmnbl=
V0vi07WkmavIrETDrDt46hzfKdJLlbZ-5Lx_3BEFcl7cnXI4dZwOzVWUDwzOyXFiT9p5gK9U">S=
onner</a>, a =E2=80=9Ctoast=E2=80=9D component). We actually use <a href=3D=
"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/Gd6OI_HGzK36rlqGSYVzRQ~~/AABEfhA~/lJlV66=
mYf3duz7noT_ZxmXeh84dHaYzDQT2xscPyrX4y_X7CjBukWCNG9oFk9mUC2n5I9EbZFg1n-thtE=
2J1_xy-8F3XqtzuZ9_gyAosJqffdqT6bHev6zuh-OtopxKo">react-hot-toast</a> here a=
t CodePen if you wanted to know (I know you did). Emil thinks it was the st=
acking animation that make it take off, of which I have no doubt. Little de=
sign polish details like that have always been a differentiator. Good desig=
n is like a cheat code. Sometimes. There is always an anti-example (looking=
 at you, Craigslist).</p>=0A<p>Weird time to talk toast components, as <a h=
ref=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/qiKkEz1ssySCqqhpogOdGw~~/AABEfhA~/=
kCS1R-OBOMuztA6ABISMkSz-qUjwJvZt5g15v__mMx2Vq7QoXjXnhBY9GXXojb7m1Wf1oQSzsOv=
qnIO-uNajLEWYUOw1lX9rgYlPfMdThk1eLMKh4T8BMDZff9qdAFrL8qdho-mwLAHDGT9QSR_1BB=
6BFawEgEEzNAmmdrnmVFgXU_YjyXwdbygqwz7RDoxG5BEsiWGMwTf_kHLVntjVgQ~~">GitHub =
has declared them always bad all the time no matter what</a>. I appreciate =
the zero nuance there. For real, it=E2=80=99s nice to just have answers in =
this complex space sometimes. I=E2=80=99m still sorting out my feelings the=
re. I feel like a little toast confirming you=E2=80=99ve saved something (f=
or example) is nice. It <em>can</em> be accessed/seen, but it doesn=E2=80=
=99t force it, as it=E2=80=99s not of huge importance. I feel like if we go=
t rid of them on CodePen, we=E2=80=99d need to replace it with some kind of=
 log or the like, which is toast-like anyway.</p>=0A<p>Similar to sharing w=
hat you do is the greatness of sharing what you <em>like</em>. David Bushel=
l <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/UQPv2iWQFAzjnar0POCIsA~~/AAB=
EfhA~/ri5yQQUbnHUkdIvi1XcEfKobfaiF5gAYuu8Po3FKy2hU_4495D-yi1sCRUyndwlQ_cUWZ=
AV8GIx9gpTWp4fOaFGsWrjtpkIIHH5v6VnI7soK613jiHGcH4XvxP6ybGflmmRD4T2UfmN7NraT=
Ab3l5ftbtvlCjS_c3RQL4609ymM~">really likes subgrid</a>. David just has a ce=
ntered single column to work with, but instead of a single <code>grid-templ=
ate-column</code>, he sets up <em>five</em>. Even if you don=E2=80=99t use =
them, you might! And now you=E2=80=99ve got these vertical alignment opport=
unities running up and down the page that you can use wherever you like, an=
d they=E2=80=99ll feel cohesive.</p>=0A<p>OK fine one more blog post archet=
ype: the sharing of a <em>good idea</em>. Here=E2=80=99s Eric Bailey with <=
a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/CwtGLf-WZnkd11cOxzjXSg~~/AABEfh=
A~/gJGsUGeJi5Mbc1JuKpjqkxyeym_oj2Ox2HXWfFAhf5e-j_-JN82Q-bK9DARvsPyUPBKxeTUM=
QoRoyTjE_SEkIQ9fPCbyLC4hyb0Q0WoLclfHBJVAdef4ipzf3NpCvIP4nUyr9SudWbEV1JoygVU=
fQQLNmeOtT4p1M_Tlg2BDXzI9IlVx6A3lvRCElij1t7D3">Accessible faux-nested inter=
active controls</a> reminding us that you <em>really really</em> shouldn=E2=
=80=99t <em>actually</em> nest interactive controls and that, good news, th=
ere is a pattern to reach for that makes them visually nested but not actua=
lly nested.</p>=0A<p>The lovely <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/=
a/RoFQEjWKfcs0FsuMuhgBDA~~/AABEfhA~/bBh0wwCTr0tubuOVtAUUKoXEK32WEe0A6dzDUtr=
LdqJWBmv0Bm9W5_6mRcEwxxq3SeoESnhjiT_SGQsx2WsohXnUoMksrSmzGrXcr38mZtKfGh5fWM=
4YhxV_m5OX5ReMkFpHQV7kPK2EImqlBhxa6Q~~">final result</a> does make me think=
 about extending the clickable area of buttons. I heard someone talk about =
recently =E2=80=9CCoyote Time=E2=80=9D but for buttons. Coyote Time refers =
to the Roadrunner cartoons, where the Coyote could run off a cliff but woul=
dn=E2=80=99t fall until he later <em>noticed</em> he had done so; the term =
was co-opted by video games. The idea is that your video game character can=
 run off a ledge <em>a little bit</em> and still be able to jump, which red=
uces how frustrated users can get at the game. What if, in that demo, the c=
lickable areas for the star and cart extended to the top and bottom of the =
rows. Maybe that makes sense=3F Maybe that=E2=80=99s Coyote Time for button=
s=3F</p>=0A<p><a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/L7c-MyS_WT--Gtu_=
JHy-rA~~/AABEfhA~/meV8_fViOdQHhIu5Hb09IqMnRh3ju7pWHgXKxG6stkRW72K7nl8Y7PaZ6=
lUzOjQvIO-9gEvvGoIsO50Y-5Gzd5sv931C9EwOpXNdlbdrIszodCwtNJ0mgTqiEiG5d3Eiu7RY=
BAgzjJ4NbqB0lpxnmPb50LbwOH7wkPkHHnopURzBePi-iEdjBrXEYhm0BJeiQeIjvhETRmkIDpH=
SWQTCxOCJZ9Tw0_ZeisOdVbVs_bI~">We should be careful</a> with how many weird=
 metaphors, acronyms, and industry slang we chuck around. If someone else r=
eads what we write, we might lose them right away!</p>=0A<blockquote><p>I f=
irst started doing Very Simple Thing with Snarfus, but the more I used it t=
he more I saw the potential! Despite the jaggle of the chromus, it=E2=80=99=
s really multi-purpose. And that=E2=80=99s what led me to argyling the pint=
afore with the quagmire instead of the hoobastank! I know, crazy.</p>=0A=09=
<cite>How I, a non-developer, read the tutorial you, a developer, wrote for=
 me, a beginner - Annie Mueller</cite>=0A</blockquote>=0D=0A               =
